Greetings, and apologies for cross-posting.

      Statistics positions at the University of California, Santa Cruz

The newly forming Department of Applied Mathematics and Statistics (AMS) of
the University of California, Santa Cruz (UCSC) invites applications for
tenured and tenure-track faculty positions for the 2002-2003 academic year.

--> We are recruiting for two positions: one at the Assistant Professor 
    level and one at any level from Assistant Professor to (full) 
    Professor. 

We seek outstanding applicants in statistics, and we particularly welcome
those with research interests in 

--> Bayesian inference, prediction, and decision-making. 

We are looking for candidates with expertise in the development of
methodological research solutions to real-world problems, in collaboration
with substantive investigators in Engineering and other campus Schools and
Departments. The campus is especially interested in candidates who can
contribute to the diversity and excellence of the academic community
through their research, teaching, and service.

The AMS Department at UCSC has only recently come into existence, with the
appointments of Professor David Draper (as the founding Chair), Assistant
Professor Raquel Prado, Visiting Associate Professor Bruno Sanso, and
Lecturer Marshall Sylvan in Statistics, and Associate Professor Neil
Balmforth, Assistant Professor Hongyun Wang, and Lecturer Hong Zhou in
Applied Mathematics.

--> The Department has a firm growth plan in place to reach 12 tenure-
    track and tenured faculty by 2005, and the prospects for substantial
    additional growth in the following years are excellent (for example,
    enrollment projections, if realized, could make possible the 
    approximate doubling of the Department between 2005 and 2010).  

It is intended that the research environment of AMS will be enlivened by a
regular stream of visitors beginning in autumn 2001 (excellent facilities
for visitors and postdocs have recently been completed), and M.S. and Ph.D.
programs will be set up in both statistics and applied mathematics as soon
as possible given the rate of growth of the faculty.

On the statistics side of AMS, the intention is to develop 

--> a fully Bayesian Statistics Group 

that will rapidly take its place as a national and international center of
excellence in Bayesian theory, methods, and applications to real-world
scientific and decision-making problems. The applied mathematics side of
the Department will emphasize topics such as dynamical systems, fluid
mechanics, chaos, biophysics, molecular biology, numerical analysis, and
parallel computing.

AMS is part of the Baskin School of Engineering at UCSC, which is entering
an exciting growth phase aimed at areas that will be in great demand in the
next decade and beyond. 

--> The Baskin School of Engineering expects to more than double in size
    over the next five to ten years. 

In addition to very strong Departments of Computer Science, Computer
Engineering and Electrical Engineering, additional programs currently under
development include Biomolecular Engineering, Software Engineering, and
Information Systems and Technology Management.

Here is one example of the collaborative opportunities for statisticians
joining AMS (this is one of many possible examples; bioinformatics is
important but is only one of a number of areas of excellence on the UCSC
campus). The Center for Biomolecular Science and Engineering (CBSE;
www.soe.ucsc.edu/centers/cbe ) at UCSC, under the leadership of Professor
David Haussler (of the Department of Computer Science), has recently played
a key role in the Human Genome Project by putting the pieces of the genome
together and starting the data analysis to discover what is in it. In
February 2001 entire special issues of Science and Nature were devoted to
the Human Genome Project, and Haussler and his colleagues played leading
roles in major papers in both issues. 

--> The CBSE is currently getting 20,000 hits a day to the web page
    (genome.ucsc.edu) on which they have published their human genome data. 

The Center is currently recruiting to fill eight positions in
bioinformatics, computational biology and chemistry, microarray and
nanotechnology, functional genomics, and molecular modeling, and the 39
faculty members in the Center, from six departments (including Computer
Science, Computer Engineering, Molecular, Cell and Developmental Biology,
Chemistry and Biochemistry, and Environmental Toxicology) are eager to
create and extend collaborations with statisticians in AMS.

We seek candidates who emphasize applications in science and engineering in
their research and will take advantage of opportunities for interactions
with colleagues in our interdisciplinary environment. In the sciences, UCSC
has internationally recognized programs in astronomy, physics, earth and
ocean sciences and molecular biology, and 

--> the Institute for Science Information (which maintains the Science and
    Social Science Citation Indices) has recently determined that UCSC's
    physical sciences departments are the second-most influential in the 
    world, based on mean research citations (per faculty member) of papers 
    published in the 1990s.

Research and instruction are supported by excellent computing facilities
and state-of-the-art laboratories. UCSC is the University of California
campus nearest to Silicon Valley and has strong ties with many high
technology companies in the area. Faculty salaries are competitive (on the
University of California's specialized salary scale for business
administration/ management and engineering, which is substantially higher
than the regular arts-and-sciences scale) and opportunities for consulting
are extensive.

MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: At the Assistant Professor level: a Ph.D. or
equivalent in statistics or a related field (Ph.D. by July 1, 2002
preferred; must be conferred within one year of appointment); demonstrated
potential for excellence in research; and a strong commitment to graduate
and undergraduate teaching. At the Associate and (full) Professor level: a
Ph.D. or equivalent in statistics or a related field; and an outstanding
record as researcher and educator, appropriate to the level of appointment.
All applicants are expected to develop externally funded research programs.

POSITIONS AVAILABLE: Fall 2002, subject to final budgetary approval.

APPLY TO: Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae; a statement of
research interests and plans; a statement of teaching interests; and 
copies or URLs of selected reprints to:

  Chair, AMS Search Committee
  Jack Baskin School of Engineering
  1156 High Street
  University of California
  Santa Cruz, CA 95064 USA

Email recruit at soe.ucsc.edu to request information on the electronic
submission of applications.

Applicants must clearly state whether they are applying for an (untenured)
Assistant Professor, a (tenured) Associate Professor, or a (full) Professor
position, and must ensure that at least four confidential letters of
recommendation are sent under separate cover. Names, affiliations, and
contact information for the references should be included with applicants'
submitted material. All letters will be treated as confidential; please
direct your references to UCSC's confidentiality statement at
http://www2.ucsc.edu/ahr/confstm.htm .

For additional information on the positions email recruit at soe.ucsc.edu
and/or David Draper <draper at ams.ucsc.edu> . For further details about the
Jack Baskin School of Engineering at UCSC, see http://www.soe.ucsc.edu/ .
The AMS web pages are under construction at present but a variety of
potentially useful information is available at 

  http://www.ams.ucsc.edu/

--> Screening will begin with applications received by December 10, 2001
    and will continue until the positions are filled (in particular, 
    applications received by January 14, 2002 will receive full
    consideration in the initial short-listing).

The scenic UC Santa Cruz campus contains 2,000 acres of redwood forest and
meadows, overlooks the Monterey Bay National Marine Sanctuary of
California's beautiful Central Coast, and is about 70 miles south of San
Francisco and 30 miles from San Jose. Additional information about UC Santa
Cruz is available at http://www.ucsc.edu/ .

UCSC IS AN AFFIRMATIVE ACTION/EQUAL EMPLOYMENT OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER; WOMEN
AND MINORITIES ARE ENCOURAGED TO APPLY. Inquiries regarding the
