From gregory_r_warnes at groton.pfizer.com  Mon Dec  3 16:32:05 2001
From: gregory_r_warnes at groton.pfizer.com (Warnes, Gregory R)
Date: Mon, 03 Dec 2001 11:32:05 -0500
Subject: Re: writing scripts in S-Plus 6 for Linux
Message-ID: <5429125E11E4D411AF7300805FE603A8014CF04A@groexmbcr02.pfizer.com>

Hi Winifred,

I think your basic misunderstanding is that S-Plus scripts are stored as
separate *files* rather than as S-plus *objects*.

Thus the 'source()' command expects a *character*string* (or an object
containing one) specifying the name of the *file*.  So instead of 

 source(test2.scr)  # broken

which tells S-Plus to go look in the *variable* names 'test2.scr' (which
doesn't exist) for a character string containing the script file name, use

 source("test2.scr")

which says go run the *file* named 'test2.scr'

2) Since S-Plus thinks (like many Unix programs) that scripts are files (not
data) it looks for scripts in the directory in which you started it, rather
than the .Data directory.   Thus you should put your script files in the
directory where you start S-Plus (or vice versa).  I.E., move the scripts to
the appropriate directory (probably just above the .Data directory).

You can, however, specify the exact location of the script by giving the
full path when specifying the filename.  I.E.: 
   > source("/home/some_directory/test2.scr")

2) The built-in 'vi()' function is really provided to allow you to edit
S-Plus objects (particularly functions) rather than scripts.  Consequently,
when vi returns it translates what you gave it into an S-plus object, rather
than as a script.   

You can, of course, use 'vi()' to create a function by 

   > myfunc <- vi()

and wrapping your two commands in 

   function() {
     <stuff here>
   }

Then execute the function by typing

   > myfunc()

3) You can also run S-Plus scripts by feeding them to Splus externally via
typing

   $  splus < test2.scr
   
or
  
   $ splus BATCH test2.scr   

at your shell prompt.

-Greg

