From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Wed Dec  5 09:22:09 2001
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Wed, 05 Dec 2001 09:22:09 +0000
Subject: Re: warning in dropping global assign
In-Reply-To: <F232wl6zO6ca67MXvyV00008413@hotmail.com>
References: <F232wl6zO6ca67MXvyV00008413@hotmail.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.31.0112050913350.22820-100000@gannet.stats>

On Wed, 5 Dec 2001, vito muggeo wrote:

> Dear all,
> I've written some function where global assignements are requested,
> for instance:
> fn<-function(....){
> x<<-....
> y<<-....
> ....
> rm(x,y, w=1)
> return(....)}
> Everything works but S+ with the output returns also
> Warning messages:
>   "1" to be removed not in database in: remove(list)
>
> The same function with "rm(x, y, pos=1)" in R works perfectly without any
> warning!!!
> Is it possible to dropt the warning? I'm running S+2000-3 for WinMe.

No: perhaps it should be made an error instead, since it is one.

You want remove(c("x", "y"), w = 1).  See ?rm and ?remove.

If you really need this (and the gurus say it is terrible S style), use

assign("x", foo, w=1, immediate=T)

but why are you using a database for temporary storage within a function?
It's normal to use frame=1.

<<- does not do the same thing in R as in S; nor does rm.  Do read the
help for the system you are actually using (!!! seems appropriate here).

