From fharrell at virginia.edu  Wed Dec  5 17:54:15 2001
From: fharrell at virginia.edu (Frank E Harrell Jr)
Date: Wed, 05 Dec 2001 12:54:15 -0500
Subject: Re: Modifying lowess to yield quantiles
In-Reply-To: <Pine.GSO.4.05.10112050750560.27965-100000@ofis450a.akctr.noaa.gov>
References: <51F9C42DA15CD311BD220008C707D81903DC83EE@usrymx10.merck.com> <Pine.GSO.4.05.10112050750560.27965-100000@ofis450a.akctr.noaa.gov>
Message-ID: <20011205125415.55386d2b.fharrell@virginia.edu>

A generalization of Duan's smearing estimator may be worth a look
(see Chapter 15 of Regression Modeling Strategies (Harrell, 2001) and
the areg.boot function in the Hmisc library).  The idea is to use a
procedure like AVAS to estimate a function g such that residuals from
  g(Y) = f1(X1) + f2(X2) + ... + fp(Xp)
are exchangeable.  The smearing estimator of quantile q of Y | X
is then g^-1(f1(X1)+...+fp(Xp)+Rq)
where Rq is the qth quantile of the vector of n residuals from the
transformation model.

Frank Harrell

