Subject: Modifying lowess to yield quantiles
Message-ID: <777F3F91D3F3D4118CCA00508B953E190195E086@usrymx06.merck.com>

Let me try resending this ...

All:

John Tukey talks about ideas like this in his classic EXPLORATORY DATA
ANALYSIS. Bill Cleveland talks about a related idea, slicing, in his book,
VISUALIZING DATA. The idea in both cases is to take (possibly overlapping)
bins of the x values and smooth quantile estimates of the y's from each.
This is not and cannot be exactly the same as lowess, which is based on
local regression -- that is, conditional expectation. If you wanted to go
that way,, I suppose you might try something like local quantile regression,
based on Koenker and Bassett's quantile regression methods, but that
probably is fairly hairy unless there is work on this that has been
published (help anyone?). 

