Subject: Re: behaviour of library function
Message-ID: <200112052052.PAA06617@nightingale.hcp.med.harvard.edu>

The behavior that Glenn points out represents (IMHO) an obsolete
construction used in the library() function, which accepts either a name
or a character string as (synonymous) arguments.  Most such constructions
are holdovers of much earlier versions of S, e.g. rm(myobject) which has
been deprecated and replaced by remove("myobject").  Besides being somewhat
inconsistent with the standard S practice of using names to represent
objects (possibly in a data.frame), it also makes it difficult to select
a library whose name is in a variable, e.g. library(myname) attaches a library
called "myname", not a library whose name is given by the character variable
myname.  The library() function does not even check that the argument is
either a string or a name, hence the ungraceful handling of the error in 
Glenn's example.

        Alan Zaslavsky

